ojwb1@cl.cam.ac.uk (O.J.W. Betts) writes:

>A new form of abuse occurs to me. I don't have 3.1 yet, so can't try this, but
>if you have checkpointing on, and you're about to get salami-ed, then crash the
>machine (probably inadvisable/difficult on multi-user machines), eg press
>RESET. Then recover the checkpoint file.
>
>Not that I advocate gratuitous save abuse as such. Personally, I modify the score
>list to indicate scores achieved using save/restore/restore/restore/...
>
>I'd be interested to hear if this works.


Well first of all, crashing a multi-user machine is inadvisable, yes.
In the present version of 3.1, there are a couple easy ways to exploit
bugs to achieve the desired effect -- either drop everything you own
except your gold and "Da" it, or hit "/n<RETURN>".
But if you can use the recover program, you can also make backups
of your save files, because recover _should_ be a utility restricted
to the game administrator, since it operates on any user's level
files without restriction.  I wrote a script to allow users to
find and recover their own crashed games, but I put in checks to
avoid cheats, like recovering a game in progress (NetHack won't
write over the first saved game, just leave the second uncompressed,
in case you *don't* die and want to continue later), and disturbing
any other user's level files.

-jim


